Today I started working on the URL extraction logic for the WebCrawler. The first thing I did was create a private function named link_extractor inside the WebCrawler class. My idea is for this function to return an array of URLs extracted from the HTML returned by the fetcher so that each URL can later be processed individually. Initially, I wrote a very simple hardcoded URL extraction logic just to verify the overall approach.

While testing the link_extractor, I needed to create a WebCrawler object in main.cpp. During compilation, I realized that my Data Structures Library was not being included in the project. To solve this, I added the Data Structures Library as a subdirectory in the WebCrawler project and modified the CMakeLists.txt files of both projects. After that, I encountered another issue where the compiler could not find linked_list.tpp, which was being used by the HashMap. This happened because the template implementation files were inside the src folder but were not part of the include directories. I fixed this by adding the src folder as an include directory for the interface library in the Data Structures Library CMake configuration.

After fixing the build issues, I encountered another bug in my DynamicArray implementation. The shrink function was using a pointer that had been declared inside an if block and later accessed outside that block. Since the pointer was out of scope, the code failed to compile. I fixed this by declaring the pointer outside the if block and initializing it with nullptr.

Once these issues were resolved, my temporary link extractor started working. It was able to extract everything after `<a href= until it encountered the closing >` character. Although this was only a temporary implementation, it confirmed that the extraction logic was working.

I then continued improving the URL extraction logic. After making some changes, the program suddenly stopped printing any URLs. After debugging the code, I found that the problem was in one of my while loop conditions. I was checking each character of the string using the logical && operator, which caused the loop to terminate as soon as one character did not match "https". Replacing && with || fixed the problem because the loop now continued until the complete "https" sequence was found. After that, I copied the URL starting from the "https" position until reaching the stopping condition.

Although this version worked, I realized it had several limitations. It only supported https links and ignored http and relative URLs. I decided to redesign the extraction logic. Instead of searching directly for "https", I started searching for the href attribute. Once href was found, I skipped characters until reaching either a single quote or double quote and then copied everything until the matching closing quote. During this implementation, I also learned how to represent a single quote character inside a character literal using '\''.

While implementing the new approach, I ran into another issue where the function produced no output. After checking the code, I discovered that the problem was simply a typo where I had accidentally written != instead of ==. After correcting it, the extractor worked much better.

The output now contained many different kinds of links, including normal web URLs as well as entries like #, mailto:, tel:, javascript:void(0), http:, and https:. This made me realize that extracting URLs alone was not enough and that I also needed a normalization step to filter invalid links and convert relative URLs into absolute ones.

Before implementing the normalizer, I tried running a basic version of the complete crawler. I wrote a simple crawl() function that fetched a page, extracted URLs, and attempted to save the downloaded HTML into the pages directory. While doing this, I found another issue because my generic Queue implementation was missing #include "queue.tpp" at the end of queue.h. After fixing that, the project compiled successfully.

During execution, I encountered another error where the fetcher threw an exception because curl_easy_perform() did not return CURLE_OK. From my understanding, this happened because many of the extracted URLs were not yet normalized and therefore could not be fetched successfully. I added exception handling around the fetch operation using a try-catch block so that the crawler would not terminate when a fetch failed.

I also noticed that the downloaded HTML pages were not appearing in the expected location. After investigating, I found that the program had created the pages directory inside the build directory because the executable was running from there.

Finally, I started designing the URL normalization component. I decided that the first step should be breaking a URL into its individual components such as scheme, host, port, path, query, and fragment. I implemented this by parsing the URL and storing each component in separate string variables.

As I thought more about URL normalization, I realized that it would become quite complex. Instead of keeping all the logic inside the WebCrawler class, I decided to create a separate URL class inside a new url_handler file. This class will be responsible for parsing URLs, resolving relative URLs into absolute ones, filtering out invalid or unsupported URLs, and performing normalization. I also moved the URL parsing code into the constructor of this class so that every URL object automatically stores its parsed components as private member variables. This design should make the crawler code cleaner and make future URL processing much easier